Two issues:

1. bags.lind: the loader flags "bare top-level machines mixed with Global block". Why? My file has `Global { ... }` block AND... are the `(Job, n) : ...` etc. being parsed as TOP-LEVEL machines instead of inside the Global block? The Global block is:
```
Global {
  {
    (Job, 3)
  }
}
```
The inner `{ (Job, 3) }` initial block parses... then bagBlockP: after initialBlockP, `endOfMachine`... initialBlockP = grouped then endOfMachine. After `}` of initial block, ws → next char '\n' → endOfMachine consumes newline. Then `}` closes Global block. Hmm, that should work.

Wait — the error means bagNames contains "Global" AND topMachs nonempty. Maybe the Global block's contents parse failed differently: `many (try (skipLines >> declInner))` — first declInner attempt at `{`... initialBlockP: grouped '{' '}' — wait, but ws at depth... The inner `{ (Job, 3) }` — grouped raises depth. sepEndBy tupleExpr ",". Then endOfMachine. OK.

Hmm, alternatively — maybe bagBlockP failed to parse `Global {` at all and... no, then there'd be no Global in bagNames.

Actually wait — maybe the issue: after the initial block inside Global, the NEXT line is `(Job, n) :` — but that's AFTER the Global block's closing `}`. Let me count braces in my file: Global { { (Job, 3) } } — line 8 `{`, 9 `(Job, 3)`, 10 `}`. ✓ closes initial; line 11 `}` closes Global. Then blank, `(Job, n) : ...` at top level. So top-level machine + Global block → correct error per my rule!

Oh! Right — I wrote the example mixing BOTH: a `Global { ... { ... } ... }` block containing only the initial block, plus bare machines at top level. Per my §11.10 decision, that's "don't mix". But is it reasonable? The user intent: bare machines = Global machines; initial block = Global initial. The rule prohibits mixing bare machines with an explicit Global BLOCK. My example has a Global block only for the initial tuples. Hmm — this reveals the rule needs nuance: is `Global { {...} }` + bare machines mixing? By the strict reading of §6 ("explicit Global is also legal — pick one style, don't mix") — yes, mixing styles. But it's a bit unfriendly...

Design decision: keep the rule strict (simple, matches handover), and fix the example to put machines inside the Global block? But then "bare top-level machines" example... Actually the cleaner example: use bare machines at top level + top-level `{ }` initial (implicit Global style), and named bags for Workers/Log/Error. That's the "implicit Global" style throughout. Let me restructure:

{
  (Job, 3)
}

(Job, n) : [...]

Workers {
  { (Salary, 10) }
  ...
}

Log { }

Error {
  (Error, rest!) : [...]
}

This is implicit-Global style, consistent. ✓ And update Workers machine indentation.

2. toy.lind: "thread blocked indefinitely" — interesting! toy.lind: initial (Add,1,2,("Print",)); machines: (Add,a,b,c) : (c!,a+b) — this machine LOOPS forever: matches Add → emits (Print, 3) → re-arms → blocks forever (no more Add tuples). Blocked forever with no exit → BlockedIndefinitely at shutdown? runProgram: main waits live<=0 — never; blocked... then GHC throws BlockedIndefinitely to blocked threads when...? Hmm, actually why did it throw instead of hanging? Because ALL threads become blocked (machines blocked on retry, main blocked on live check, runner blocked on queue retry) → RTS detects total blockage → throws. Previously (pre-loader) toy.lind was never RUN by Main (only parsed), so this never surfaced. It's expected semantics: a program with no exit path deadlocks. GHC kills it with BlockedIndefinitely. Exit code 1 with an ugly message.

Is that acceptable? The handover says such programs need exit/die paths. A nicer CLI story: catch BlockedIndefinitely and report "program deadlocked: all machines blocked" — informative. Let me do that in Main: catch BlockedIndefinitely from runLoaded, print message, exit with failure? Or should the RTS own this? Provisional: Main catches and prints "lindana: all machines blocked; program deadlocked (no exit path)". House style: provisional decisions documented. I'll add it in Main (Simplehc: Control.Exception catch, BlockedIndefinitely from Control.Exception).

Actually better in Machine.runLoaded? The RTS returning a distinguished ExitCode? Hmm — RunResult.rrExit ExitFailure 1 + maybe a field? Simplest: Main catches. But test suites might want it. Keep in Main for now, note in handover.

Fix example + Main.

